// Lesson 7.4 — Ladders II: Mounts, Tops & Polish
// The top-of-ladder problem, the mount decision, and an HONEST climb-animation
// block: Player_Shovel.controller holds only Idle / Walk / Shovel_Swing x
// Up / Horiz / Down. There are no climb clips and no climb frames in any
// character sheet, so adding climb animation is framed as "here is what you
// would have to do", never as something already present.
// Nobody has made the capped-ladder decision for the student - the starter
// contains no ladders at all.
//
// SCAFFOLDING: OUTLINE ONLY (level 3). 7.3 built the climb mechanism - the
// trigger zone, the flag and the branch in Update - and this lesson is its
// edge cases: the mount decision, the arrival check at the top, and the polish.
// Nothing here is a new mechanism, so nothing here is handed over as code: the
// ask banner is the level marker and there is no walkthrough (verify-site
// refuses a level-3 page that renders one - see the `level >= 3 && wt` rule).
//
// The banner text is copied character for character from the 7.4 entry in
// src/data/asks.ts, because verify-site compares the ladder level the index
// claims against the level this page renders.
//
// The recurring three-beat block is ADDED, not substituted: it opens the
// 7.4:dc1:qN answer-id namespace and leaves every protected block alone, so
// the socratic, bug-hunt and tinker boxes keep the ids they already had
// (src/lib/formify.ts ids are positional - nothing is reordered or re-counted).
//
// GROUND TRUTH (read out of AP_CS_P_Starter_Project, Unity 6000.6.0f1):
//   - The ladder is a TILEMAP picture, and 7.3 gives the student three places
//     to paint it: the `No Collision` tilemap (renderer only - it carries a
//     TilemapRenderer and NO collider of any kind), a new renderer-only tilemap
//     child, or the solid `Collision` tilemap (which stops the player dead).
//     A picture painted on a collider-less layer cannot be found by a trigger
//     or a raycast at all, which is why 7.3 pairs the art with a separate
//     trigger ZONE object - the zone is what the code talks to, never the
//     tiles. This page never asserts which of the three the student chose.
//   - `Collision` is genuinely solid: TilemapCollider2D + CompositeCollider2D +
//     Rigidbody2D Body Type Static, and 7.2 has the student set Composite
//     Operation to Merge. `Cave Exit` is the same three components over two
//     tiles. `No Collision` has the renderer and nothing else.
//   - TagManager.asset: tags are Coin, Enemy, Axe, Spikes, Ground, GroundCheck
//     - there is still no `Ground` LAYER until the student creates one in 7.2,
//     which is why the arrival check reuses 7.2's repaired mask rather than a
//     new one. Sorting layers are Default, Bottom, Layer 2, Player, Layer 4, Top.
//   - Physics2DSettings.asset: gravity y = -9.81, m_QueriesStartInColliders: 1
//     (a query that begins inside a collider reports that collider).
//   - The HUD built in 5.3 lives in `Start`, which is build index 0. Only
//     Overworld ships a Canvas; Start and Platformer contain none, so the HUD
//     genuinely does not exist in the cave. The prompt claim below stands.
//   - Player_Shovel.controller holds exactly nine states - Idle / Walk /
//     Shovel_Swing, each in _Up / _Horiz / _Down - driven by IsWalking (Bool),
//     WalkDir (Int) and Attack (Trigger), the three parameters 1.6 built.
//     No climb clip or climb pose exists anywhere in Assets/.
import Lesson from '../../layouts/LessonLayout.astro';
import { renderAskBanner } from '../../lib/walkthrough';
import { renderDecompose } from '../../lib/decompose';
import type { DecomposeSpec } from '../../lib/decompose';

// The three recurring questions, at level 3: the parts are named and sourced
// and what each one DOES is the student's to say. Every doc pointer is a
// lesson the student has finished (1.4, 5.3, 6.1, 6.2, 6.3, 7.1, 7.2, 7.3)
// and nothing later than today - the "connect it" beat may point backward only.
const DECOMPOSE: DecomposeSpec = {
  big:
    'A climb that starts when the player means it to and stops when the player has arrived — ' +
    'at the bottom, at the top, and everywhere the trigger zone and the ladder picture disagree ' +
    'about where either of those is.',
  parts: [
    {
      name: 'Say when the climb starts',
      does: 'The mount rule as one sentence before it is a line of code: pressing up inside the zone, being grabbed the moment you walk in, or only when both are true on the same frame. The zone sends the same two messages whichever you pick, so the choice costs nothing in the script and everything in the feel.',
      doc: '7.3 — the trigger zone and the two callbacks it sends',
      href: '7.3.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'List every way the climb can end',
      does: 'Reached the top, jumped off, died, walked back out of the zone. Four exits, one flag — and the one you leave out is the one a tester finds inside a minute: a player standing on the floor with the climb state still switched on.',
      doc: '7.3 — the climb flag on PlayerController, and every place that reads it',
      href: '7.3.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Ask "have I arrived?" with the ground check you already own',
      does: 'The arrival test is the question 6.2 already asks, pointed down from a player who is moving upward. A second way of asking whether there is floor under your feet is a second answer, and the two will disagree on exactly the frame that decides the state.',
      doc: '6.2 — the ground check you chose: raycast, overlap circle, or touching layers',
      href: '6.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Re-tune that check for a player who is climbing',
      does: 'The walking check was tuned for feet already on the floor. A climber needs a different reach and the same Ground mask. Too short and the climb never ends; too long and it ends with a whole ladder still above you, because the floor at the bottom of the shaft came into range.',
      doc: '7.2 — the Ground layer you made, and the mask you repaired to use it',
      href: '7.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Decide where a climb with nothing to arrive at ends',
      does: 'Not every ladder has a ledge above it. Capping it answers the question with level design instead of code: one solid tile stops the player, and the last step becomes the jump you tuned in 6.1. The cap goes on the solid tilemap — the picture-only layer has no collider to stop anybody.',
      doc: '7.2 — the Collision tilemap, and the paint-on-the-right-layer decision',
      href: '7.2.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Order the arrival check against the climb movement',
      does: 'Two branches in one Update, and one order between them. Move first and the player drifts into the tile above for exactly one frame; check first and the state is already back to normal before anything else touches the body.',
      doc: 'the climb branch you wrote into Update in 7.3, on top of the movement from 1.4',
      href: '7.3.html',
    },
    {
      name: 'Make the mount readable, or decide it does not need to be',
      does: 'A ladder that looks climbable is a complete answer and costs nothing. If you add a prompt, the coin rule applies: it dies the instant it is obeyed. It is a Console line unless you build a world-space object, because the HUD from 5.3 lives in the scene the game begins in and does not follow you into the cave.',
      doc: '5.3 — the Canvas HUD, and the one scene that owns it',
      href: '5.3.html',
    },
  ],
  why:
    'Every part above can be answered out loud, in one sentence, before the script is open — and that ' +
    'is the whole test. The climb mechanism already exists and does not change; what fails at the ends ' +
    'of a ladder is a handful of independent decisions that all wear the same symptom until they are ' +
    'separated. The reach of the arrival check, the order of two branches inside one Update, and the ' +
    'frame the exit runs on are three different failures with three different fixes, and a student who ' +
    'treats them as one thing called "the ladder is broken" changes all three at once and learns nothing ' +
    'from the result.',
  sizeTest:
    'The two-minute test: can you answer the part in one sentence without opening Unity? "Where the ' +
    'climb ends when there is no ledge above it" passes. "Fix the top of the ladder" does not — a part ' +
    'that names a symptom instead of a decision is not a part yet, it is the problem again in fewer words.',
  turns: [
    'Two exits can want to fire on the same frame — the cap stops the player and the arrival check agrees there is floor beneath them. Say which one you want to win, and describe what the player sees during the frame it takes to find out.',
    'A capped ladder moves the last step out of your script and into level design. Name one thing that gets easier because of that, and one thing a tester can break that no line of code can protect against.',
    'The bottom of a ladder is the mirror of the top: the zone ends and the player is standing on nothing. Which of your exits covers that case today, and what does the player see if none of them does?',
  ],
  connect: [
    'In 7.3 the zone sent two messages and wrote a flag on the player. Which part above is a third writer of that same flag — and why does every exit have to write it in one place instead of wherever it happens?',
    'In 7.2 you made the layers this project never had and repaired a ground-check mask that had been asking about a layer that did not exist. Which part above reuses that repaired mask, and what was the broken version really testing against?',
    'In 6.2 the ground check answered its question every frame, and in 6.3 you added a window to the same comparison. Which part above is that question asked at a new moment — and why is reusing it cheaper than writing a second one that agrees with the first?',
    'In 6.1 you tuned the jump, and in 7.1 you snapped the cave to a grid. Which part above makes both of those numbers load-bearing at the same time — and what happens to your ladders the week you retune the jump?',
    'In 5.3 the HUD was built in the scene the game begins in, so it does not exist in the cave at all. Which part above has to live with that — and what does it use instead of a label on screen?',
  ],
};

const body = `<h1>7.4 — Ladders II: Mounts, Tops &amp; Polish</h1>

<p class="lesson-lead">
    Your ladder works in the middle and fails at both ends. Climb it and keep holding W: you
    rise into the ceiling, or hover at the seam, or fall off the moment the zone ends. The
    middle of a ladder is easy. The last three tiles are the hardest problem in platformer
    engineering, and nobody has solved them for you — there are no ladders in this project
    except the one you built in <a href="7.3.html">7.3</a>.
</p>

${renderAskBanner('how do I stop my ladder breaking at the top and bottom?', 3)}

${renderDecompose('7.4', DECOMPOSE, 3)}

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity discovery">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">The Problem: The Top of the Ladder</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Climb your ladder from <a href="7.3.html">7.3</a> and hold W past the top tile. Write
        down exactly what happens — every build does something slightly different, and yours is
        the only build that matters. Then read the three classic answers:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Answer</th><th>What the player feels</th><th>What it costs you</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Stop at the top.</strong> While climbing, ask whether there is floor under your feet; if there is, leave the climb state and stand up.</td>
            <td>Honest and sudden. The climb ends the moment you have arrived.</td>
            <td>One more physics question per frame, and a precise definition of "at the top".</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Pop off.</strong> Holding W past the top applies a small upward hop that carries you onto the ledge.</td>
            <td>Flows straight into a jump. Can feel generous or magical.</td>
            <td>Fiddly physics. The hop can overshoot into a spike or an enemy you never saw.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Cap the ladder.</strong> Paint a solid tile above the top of the strip so the player simply stops under it, then jumps the last bit.</td>
            <td>Blunt and completely learnable. Every player reads it instantly.</td>
            <td>Level design must guarantee the last jump is possible with the jump you tuned in <a href="6.1.html">6.1</a>. Zero new code.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <div class="callout info">
        <strong>The cheapest answer is a tile, not a script.</strong> Option three requires no new
        code at all — it is one painted tile on the <code>Collision</code> tilemap, and it works
        because <a href="7.2.html">7.2</a> already made that tilemap solid. Before you write
        anything, check whether your level can simply be shaped to solve the problem. A great deal
        of platformer engineering is avoided by moving one tile.
    </div>

    <div class="socratic">
        <h3>Socratic: Who Decides Where the Climb Ends?</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Which of the three answers does your build currently have — by accident, not by choice? Say what the player sees at the top tile right now.</li>
            <li>Option three costs no code and option one costs a per-frame physics question. Which one teaches the player more about your game's rules, and which one is easier to explain to a tester?</li>
            <li>If you cap the ladder, the last step is a jump. What must be true about the height between the cap and the ledge above it? Which number from <a href="6.1.html">6.1</a> — or which pair of numbers — decides it?</li>
            <li>If you stop at the top, you must define "at the top" precisely. Is that "there is ground 0.2 units below me", or "I have risen above the ladder's first tile", or something else? Which definition survives a player who climbs halfway and lets go?</li>
            <li>Your ladder zone is a box. The top of the box and the top of the picture are two different places. Which one should end the climb, and what goes wrong if they disagree?</li>
            <li>A ladder that ends with a jump can strand a player who is out of practice. Is stranding ever acceptable? What level feature would you add to make it safe?</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Read Function-Level Examples</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        Each of these is a machine that has to know when to stop. Read each one for what it does
        in isolation, then find the piece that maps onto the top of your ladder.
    </p>

    <p><strong>Example 1 — A lift levelling itself with a landing, in a hoist controller:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The car stops when its floor lines up with the landing floor,
// not at the end of the shaft.
if (Mathf.Abs(carFloorY - landingY) &lt; tolerance) StopCar();</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 2 — A crane's limit switch, cutting the motor at the top of the arc:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>private bool atLimit;   // set by the switch, cleared when the hook descends</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 3 — An escalator's comb plate at the top landing:</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The same tread that carried you up lets you walk off level.
// Nothing lifts you - you simply stop being carried.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 4 — An aircraft boarding stair's handrail (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The rail is only a rail while someone is on the steps. It is
// not a wall, and it never appears in the doorway.</code></pre>

    <p><strong>Example 5 — A stage's fly floor and its counterweight (comment only):</strong></p>
    <pre><code>// The rope takes the weight, the floor takes it back. The moment
// is a handover, and handovers are where the bugs live.</code></pre>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>Architectural specification: ending the climb cleanly.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li>Which question do you ask to detect "I have arrived"? Name the exact API you already own from <a href="6.2.html">6.2</a> — you built a ground check there, in whatever form you chose. Reuse it; do not write a second one.</li>
            <li>How far down does that question need to reach while you are climbing? Your normal ground check distance was tuned for walking. What number would miss the ledge, and what does an over-long reach risk detecting instead?</li>
            <li>What is the very first thing that must happen when you decide the climb is over — the state flag, the gravity, or the movement? Justify the order.</li>
            <li>Which branch of your <code>Update</code> runs first: the climb movement or the arrival check? If the movement runs first on the frame you arrive, what does the player do for one frame, and does anyone see it?</li>
            <li>When you leave the climb state at the top, do you snap the player onto the ledge or let physics settle them? Argue both, then choose.</li>
            <li>How do you know you have not broken the ordinary ground check for walking? Name the test you will run after the change.</li>
        </ul>
    </div>

    <div class="callout trap">
        <strong>The order-of-operations bug will not announce itself.</strong> If your climb branch
        moves the player <em>before</em> you check whether the climb has ended, then on the arrival
        frame you move once more and then stop — a single frame of drift into the tile above. At
        60 frames per second that is a millimetre. At a low frame rate, on a slow machine, it is
        enough to shove the player inside a collider that then pushes them out sideways. Find the
        order in your own <code>Update</code>, write it down, and test at a deliberately low frame
        rate before you decide it does not matter.
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Build Lab: Mount, Cap, and What a Climb Would Take</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~30 min</span>
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 1 — solve the top.</strong> Choose one of the three answers and build it.
        You have already read the costs. If you choose the capped ladder, paint the cap tile
        first and test the last jump before you write a line of code — if the jump does not clear
        it, the answer is to move the cap, not to add a pop-off.
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 2 — the mount decision.</strong> Walk into a ladder and nothing happens until
        you press W. That is one design. Another is that stepping into the zone mounts you
        automatically and you hang there. A third is that the ladder only grabs you if you press W
        <em>while inside the zone</em>, so walking past it sideways never snags you. Read all
        three, choose one, and answer the question that decides it: <em>what does the player
        expect when they walk past a ladder on the ground?</em>
    </p>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 3 — the mount prompt, or its absence.</strong> The bottom of a tall ladder
        can be out of reach or hard to read. Three honest options, and the project supports all
        of them:
    </p>

    <table>
        <tr><th>Prompt</th><th>What the player sees</th><th>What it costs</th></tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>Nothing at all</strong> — make the ladder obvious through geometry and tiles</td>
            <td>A ladder that looks climbable</td>
            <td>Nothing. And it is the option real games choose most often.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A <code>Debug.Log</code> line</strong> while the mount is possible</td>
            <td>The Console, which your player cannot see</td>
            <td>Nothing. The HUD you built in 5.3 lives in <code>Start</code> and shows your own stats — it is not in this scene and does not know a ladder exists, so anything you want to watch here is still a Console line.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td><strong>A world-space text object</strong> (a TextMesh in the scene) toggled active while the mount is possible</td>
            <td>Floating text, in the world, above the ladder</td>
            <td>A new object, a new reference, and a rule about when it disappears.</td>
        </tr>
    </table>

    <p>
        Whatever you choose, the rule is the same one you applied to the spike and the coin:
        <strong>a prompt dies the instant it is obeyed or no longer relevant.</strong> A prompt
        that lingers after you have started climbing is worse than no prompt.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
        <strong>What adding a real climb animation would actually involve.</strong> You may have
        noticed your character rises up the ladder in a standing pose. That is not an oversight —
        it is the honest state of the project. The Player's Animator controller contains exactly
        three motions, <strong>Idle</strong>, <strong>Walk</strong> and
        <strong>Shovel_Swing</strong>, and each of those exists in <strong>Up</strong>,
        <strong>Horizontal</strong> and <strong>Down</strong> variants, driven by the
        <code>IsWalking</code>, <code>WalkDir</code> and <code>Attack</code> parameters you built
        in Unit 1. There is no climb state, no climb clip, and <strong>no climbing pose in any
        sprite sheet that ships with this project</strong> — check
        <code>Assets/Artwork/Character/Player/</code> and its
        <code>ExtraSpriteSheets/</code> folder if you want to confirm that yourself.
        <br><br>
        So if you want a climb animation, here is the whole job, in order, and none of it is
        today's work:
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Get frames.</strong> Either draw climbing poses, or find a licensed sheet. This is the step that stops most students, and it is art, not code.</li>
            <li><strong>Import and slice</strong> the sheet in the Sprite Editor — the same workflow you met in Unit 1 — so each pose is its own sprite.</li>
            <li><strong>Author two clips</strong>, a climb-idle and a climb-move, or one clip for both if you are being economical.</li>
            <li><strong>Add the states</strong> to the controller and wire transitions in and out of them.</li>
            <li><strong>Add a parameter</strong> — a Bool named for climbing — and drive it from the same state flag your trigger zone already writes.</li>
            <li><strong>Decide the Up/Down question:</strong> do you need a mirrored clip, or does one climb pose read correctly in both directions?</li>
        </ul>
        If you do decide to take that on later, it belongs to <a href="7.7.html">7.7</a>'s polish
        pass or to Unit 8 — not to a mechanic that already works.
    </div>

    <p>
        <strong>Step 4 — polish passes. Pick two, and no more.</strong>
        <ul>
            <li><strong>Ladder edges:</strong> at the very bottom of the climb state, restore a moment of gravity so the player settles onto the floor instead of hanging in the air when the zone ends.</li>
            <li><strong>Ladders through one-way ledges:</strong> if you built a one-way platform in <a href="7.2.html">7.2</a>, a climb can pass up through the cap. The two features love each other — test it and write down what happens.</li>
            <li><strong>Sound:</strong> one soft tick per tile climbed. You are not adding audio today unless you already have a clip; note it as a candidate instead.</li>
            <li><strong>The re-entry repair from <a href="7.3.html">7.3</a>:</strong> if you chose the cooldown, tune its length. Time it with a stopwatch, not by feel.</li>
        </ul>
    </p>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Bug Hunt: Both Ends</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~15 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="bug-hunt">
        <h3>Bug Hunt</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>At the top you exit the climb while still holding W — but your movement code runs before the arrival check. What happens on the frame after the exit, and how would you prove it is only one frame?</li>
            <li>The prompt appears when you are near the ladder's base, and you jump up into the zone from below. Should the prompt have appeared mid-jump? State the rule you would use instead of "prompt whenever possible".</li>
            <li>Two ladders stacked with a one-tile gap between them. Draw the enter and exit messages on a timeline as the player climbs through the seam. Which message arrives last, and what is the flag's value afterwards?</li>
            <li>You capped the ladder, but the cap sits four tiles above the floor and the ledge is one tile above that. Can you still finish the climb? What number from 6.1 decides it, and what do you change — the cap or the jump?</li>
            <li>Your arrival check reaches too far down and finds the floor at the <em>bottom</em> of the ladder. Describe the symptom — the player is still climbing, but the state has already ended. How do you narrow the check without breaking the walking ground check?</li>
            <li>Set your arrival check to reuse the 6.2 ground check exactly as written, mask and all. It never fires while climbing. Why not? (Think about which layer the ladder picture is on and which layer the cap tile is on.)</li>
        </ol>
    </div>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ -->
<div class="activity basic-app">
    <div class="activity-header">
        <span class="activity-label">Tinker</span>
        <span class="activity-time">~10 min</span>
    </div>

    <div class="tinker">
        <h3>Tinker: Ladder Levels</h3>
        <ol>
            <li>Design a chimney: two ladders three tiles apart with enemies patrolling the top. What is the player reading during the climb — and why is climbing a perfect vulnerability window, given that you cannot dodge sideways mid-climb?</li>
            <li>Make one route up a wall a ladder and the other route a set of jumps. Time both with a stopwatch. Which does a tester choose first, and which do they use on the second attempt? Record it — this is <a href="9.5.html">9.5</a> data in the wild.</li>
            <li>Build a ladder whose top is capped and whose bottom is a two-tile drop. Does anything about the ladder need to change for the drop, or is the drop entirely the floor's business?</li>
            <li>Take the mount prompt you chose and delete it entirely for one playtest. Does the tester still find the ladder? What does their behaviour tell you about whether a prompt was ever doing any work?</li>
            <li>Time a climb with a stopwatch at two different <code>climbSpeed</code> values and write both numbers next to the ladder's height in tiles. Which one would you defend to a tester, and why?</li>
            <li>Commit and push: <code>"7.4: ladder tops + mount decision + polish"</code>.</li>
        </ol>
    </div>

    <div class="checklist">
        <h3>Checkpoint — before moving on, you must be able to:</h3>
        <ul>
            <li>Explain the top-of-ladder problem and name the three classic answers with a cost for each</li>
            <li>Say which answer you built, and why it fits your level</li>
            <li>Reuse your 6.2 ground check to end the climb, and explain why the reach had to change</li>
            <li>Defend your mount rule and your prompt decision — including choosing no prompt</li>
            <li>State honestly that this project ships no climb frames, and list what adding one would involve</li>
            <li>Break this task into parts small enough to look each one up on its own, and say in one line what each part does</li>
            <li>Say which earlier lesson each part of this task came from, and where you would reread it</li>
            <li>Commit and push</li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

